// GAP-ZYB-012 — reading a digit back, in any of the 69 scripts the language
// already writes.
//
// `#|…|` is the safe conversion to a number, and it knew every digit script
// already — but only through a String. The same character written as a Char
// came back untouched: `#|"७"|` was 7 and `#|'७'|` was `७`. Even `#|'7'|`
// handed back the Char rather than the number. One operator, one character,
// two answers depending on which of the two ways it had been written — the
// asymmetry with no written rule that also cost BUG-ZYB-002 and GAP-ZYB-008.
//
// The report said the language "writes 69 scripts and cannot read any", and
// that a program had to declare the zero of every script it accepted. It does
// not: `#|c|` gives the value. The probe had used `##!` — the code point —
// which is a different question, and is the fifth finding in this log to
// have tested the operator NEXT TO the one that answers.
//
// `<<|` delivers a Char, so this is the shape a keyboard actually produces.
valor(c) { <~ #|c| }
es_digito(c) { <~ ((#|c|)#?)[1] == "###" }

>> "ascii:      " valor('7') ¶
>> "devanagari: " valor('७') ¶
>> "árabe:      " valor('٣') ¶
>> "canarés:    " valor('೯') ¶
>> "bengalí:    " valor('৫') ¶
>> "tailandés:  " valor('๘') ¶

// Not a digit: handed back untouched, which is what "safe conversion" already
// meant for a string.
>> "letra:      " valor('z') ¶
>> "punto:      " valor('.') ¶

>> "es_digito('७') = " es_digito('७') ¶
>> "es_digito('z') = " es_digito('z') ¶

// And the String form still reads the same, which is the point of the fix.
s = "७"
>> "por cadena: " #|s| ¶
